@c bib -> itexi intro
@itemize

@item
Harald Banter.
@strong{Akkord-Lexikon}.
Schott's Söhne, Mainz, Germany, 1987 (in German).
Comprehensive overview of commonly used chords. Suggests (and uses) a
  unification for all different kinds of chord names.




@item
A.@tie{}Beverly Barksdale.
@strong{The Printed Note: 500 Years of Music Printing and Engraving}.
The Toledo Museum of Art, Toledo, Ohio, January 1957.
“The exhibition ‘The Printed Note’ attempts to show the various processes
  used since the second of the 15th century for reproducing music mechanically
  ...”. The illustrations mostly feature ancient music. It is available as a
  reprint from Da Capo Press, New York (1981).




@item
Laszlo Boehm.
@strong{Modern Music Notation}.
G. Schirmer, Inc., New York, 1961.
Heussenstamm writes: A handy compact reference book in basic notation.




@item
H.@tie{}Elliot Button.
@strong{System in Musical Notation}.
Novello & Co, Ltd., London, 1920.





@item
Herbert Chlapik.
@strong{Die Praxis des Notengraphikers}.
Doblinger, 1987 (in German).
A clearly written book for the casually interested reader. It shows some of the
  conventions and difficulties in printing music (HWN).




@item
Anthony Donato.
@strong{Preparing Music Manuscript}.
Prentice-Hall, Englewood Cliffs, NJ, 1963.





@item
Donemus.
@strong{Uitgeven van muziek}: Handleiding voor componisten en copiisten.
Donemus Amsterdam, 1982 (in Dutch).
Manual on copying for composers and copyists at the Dutch publishing house
  Donemus. Besides general comments on copying, it also contains a lot of
  hands-on advice for making performance material for modern pieces.




@item
William Gamble.
@strong{Music Engraving and Printing}: Historical and Technical Treatise.
Sir Isaac Pitman & Sons, Ltd., 1923.
This patriotic book was an attempt to promote and help British music engravers.
  It is somewhat similar to Hader's book [hader48] in scope and style, but
  Gamble focuses more on technical details (which French punch cutters are
  worth buying from, etc.), and does not treat typographical details, such as
  optical illusions. It is available as a reprint from Da Capo Press, New York
  (1971).




@item
Tom Gerou and Linda Lusk.
@strong{Essential Dictionary of Music Notation}.
Alfred Publishing, Van Nuys CA, 1996.
A cheap, concise, alphabetically ordered list of typesetting and music
  (notation) issues with a rather simplistic attitude but in most cases
  “good-enough” answers (JCN).




@item
Elaine Gould.
@strong{Behind Bars}.
Faber Music Ltd., 2011.
A comprehensive guide to the rules and conventions of music notation. Covering
  everything from basic themes to complex techniques and providing a
  comprehensive grounding in notational principles.




@item
Elaine Gould.
@strong{Hals über Kopf}.
Edition Peters, 2014 (in German, translated by Arne Muus and Jens Berger).
Ein vollständiges Regelwerk für den Modernen Notensatz auf höchstem Niveau,
  ein Meilenstein der Notationskunde und ein Leitfaden für jeden Musiker, der
  sich auf diesem Gebiet umfassende Kenntnisse aneignen möchte.




@item
Karl Hader.
@strong{Aus der Werkstatt eines Notenstechers}.
Verlag Waldheim-Eberle, Vienna, 1948 (in German).
Hader was a chief engraver in a Viennese engraving workshop. This beautiful
  booklet was intended as an introduction for laymen on the art of engraving.
  It contains a step by step, in-depth explanation of how to cut and stamp
  music into zinc plates. It also contains a few compactly formulated rules on
  musical orthography. Out of print.




@item
George Heussenstamm.
@strong{The Norton Manual of Music Notation}.
Norton, New York, 1987.
Hands-on instruction book for copying (i.e., handwriting) music. Fairly
  complete (HWN).




@item
Klaus Ignatzek.
@strong{Die Jazzmethode für Klavier 1}.
Schott, 1995 (in German).
This book contains a system for denoting chords that is used in LilyPond.




@item
Andreas Jaschinski, editor.
@strong{Notation}.
Number BVK1625. Bärenreiter-Verlag, 2000 (in German).
Articles from “Musik in Geschichte und Gegenwart” pertaining to notation.
  Both ‘alternative’ notation and ancient notation is treated in depth,
  while ‘common’ notation is not.




@item
Harold@tie{}M. Johnson.
@strong{How to write music manuscript}: An exercise-method handbook for the
  music student, copyist, arranger, composer, teacher.
Carl Fischer, Inc., New York, 1946.





@item
Erhard Karkoschka.
@strong{Notation in New Music}: A Critical Guide to Interpretation and
  Realisation.
Praeger Publishers, New York, 1972 (translated by Ruth Koenig).
Out of print.




@item
М.@tie{}Е. Кунин.
@strong{Из истории нотопечатания}.
Издательство “Советский композитор”, 1963
  (in Russian).
[M. E. Kunin. From the history of music score printing. Publisher “Soviet
  composer”, Moscow 1963.] The book outlines the history of music printing
  from Octavianus Scotus' experiments in 1482 (Venice), to Simon Gutovsky in
  1679 (Moscow), and those in Russia who were after him: Stellovsky, Bessel,
  Yurgenson and Belyaev (among others). It then describes how music printing
  developed in the USSR. The photos illustrate the methods of music printing
  that were in use in the USSR in the sixties: the metal plate engraving,
  stamping, and a patended method called “notonabor”, together with its
  slight upgrade 20 years later.




@item
М.@tie{}Е. Кунин.
@strong{Нотопечатание}.
Издательство “Музыка”, 1966 (in Russian).
[M. E. Kunin. Music score printing. Publisher “Music”, Moscow 1966.] This
  book is a new edition of [kunin63], with different photos.




@item
Mark McGrain.
@strong{Music notation}.
Hal Leonard Publishing Corporation, 1991.
Obviously a ‘book’ edition of lecture notes. The book looks like it is
  xeroxed from bad printouts. The content has nothing you won't find in other
  books like [read79] or [heussenstamm87] (HWN).




@item
Music@tie{}Publishers'@tie{}Association of@tie{}the United@tie{}States.
@strong{Standard music notation specifications for computer programming}.
December 1996.
Pamphlet explaining a few fine points in music font design (HWN).




@item
Richard Rastall.
@strong{The Notation of Western Music: an Introduction}.
J. M. Dent & Sons London, 1983.
Interesting account of the evolution and origin of common notation starting
  from neumes, and ending with modern innovations (HWN).




@item
Gardner Read.
@strong{Modern Rhythmic Notation}.
Indiana University Press, 1978.
Sound (boring) review of the various hairy rhythmic notations used by
  avant-garde composers (HWN).




@item
Gardner Read.
@strong{Music Notation: a Manual of Modern Practice}.
Taplinger Publishing, New York, Second edition, 1979.
This is as close to the “standard” reference work for music notation issues
  as one is likely to get.




@item
Clinton Roemer.
@strong{The Art of Music Copying}.
Roerick music co., Sherman Oaks (CA), Second edition, 1984.
Out of print. Heussenstamm writes: an instructional manual which specializes in
  methods used in the commercial field.




@item
Glen Rosecrans.
@strong{Music Notation Primer}.
Passantino, New York, 1979.
Heussenstamm writes: Limited in scope, similar to [Roemer84].




@item
Carl@tie{}A. Rosenthal.
@strong{A Practical Guide to Music Notation}: for Composers, Arrangers and
  Editors.
MCA Music, New York, 1967.
Heussenstamm writes: Informative in terms of traditional notation. Does not
  concern score preparation.




@item
Ted Ross.
@strong{Teach yourself the art of music engraving and processing}: a complete
  manual, reference and text book on preparing music for reproduction and
  print.
Hansen House, Miami, Florida, Third edition, 1987.
This is about engraving, i.e., professional typesetting. It contains directions
  on good typesetting, but the sections on reproduction technicalities, how to
  use pens and history are interesting. Especially the section on Music
  Typewriters is amusing (HWN).




@item
Schirmer.
@strong{The G. Schirmer Manual/AMP of Style and Usage}.
G. Schirmer Inc., New York, Fourth edition, 2009.
Expanded and updated by David Fetherolf. This is the style guide for Schirmer
  publications. This manual specifically focuses on preparing print for
  publication by Schirmer. It discusses many details that are not in other,
  normal notation books. It also gives a good idea of what is necessary to
  bring printouts to publication quality. It can be ordered from the rental
  department.




@item
Kurt Stone.
@strong{Music Notation in the Twentieth Century}: A Practical Guidebook.
Norton, New York, 1980.
Heussenstamm writes: The most important book on notation in recent years.




@item
Börje Tyboni.
@strong{Noter}: Handbok i traditionell notering.
Gehrmans Musikförlag, Stockholm, 1996 (in Swedish).
Swedish book on music notation.




@item
Albert@tie{}C. Vinci.
@strong{Fundamentals of Traditional Music Notation}.
Kent State University Press, 1989.





@item
Helene Wanske.
@strong{Musiknotation}: Von der Syntax des Notenstichs zum EDV-gesteuerten
  Notensatz.
Schott-Verlag, Mainz, 1988 (in German).
I. A very thorough overview of engraving practices of various craftsmen. It
  includes detailed specs of characters, dimensions, etc. II. A thorough
  overview of an anonymous (by now antiquated) automated system. EDV means
  ‘e(lektronische) D(aten)v(erarbeitung)’, electronic data processing
  (HWN).




@item
Maxwell Weaner and Walter Boelke.
@strong{Standard Music Notation Practice}.
Music Publisher's Association of the United States Inc., New York, 1993.
Revised edition by Arnold Broido and Daniel Dorff.




@item
Johannes Wolf.
@strong{Handbuch der Notationskunde}.
Breitkopf & Härtel, Leipzig, 1919 (in German).
Very thorough treatment (in two volumes) of the history of music notation.




@end itemize
@c bib -> itexi end
